<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karl Friedrich Schinkel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1781">1781</a>–<a href="/years/1841">1841</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karl Friedrich Schinkel was a Prussian architect, city planner and painter, born in Neuruppin, and a leader of the Neoclassical and Gothic Revival movements. A student of Friedrich Gilly and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Gilly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, he earned his living as a painter and worked for the stage, creating in 1816 a backdrop for Mozart's The Magic Flute. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s defeat he oversaw the Prussian Building Commission and reshaped Berlin as a capital for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Turning to Greek rather than Imperial Roman models, he built the Neue Wache, the Schauspielhaus at the Gendarmenmarkt and the Altes Museum on Museum Island (1823–1830). He died in Berlin in 1841.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-friedrich-schinkel.jpg" alt="Karl Friedrich Schinkel" width="330" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Joseph Begas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schinkel1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Schinkel was born on 13 March 1781 in Neuruppin, in the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. When he was six his father died in the Neuruppin fire of 1787. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> he became a student of Friedrich Gilly, who was nine years his elder, and the two became close friends.</p><p>After returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> from his first trip to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1805, Schinkel began to earn his living as a painter. At the Berlin art exhibition of 1810 he saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspar-david-friedrich">Caspar David Friedrich</a>'s painting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanderer-above-the-sea-of-fog">Wanderer above the Sea of Fog</a>, decided that he would never reach such mastery, and turned to architecture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Between 1808 and 1817 he renovated Schloss Rosenau at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> in the Gothic Revival style, and he also rebuilt the ruins of Chorin Abbey. For the stage he created the star-spangled backdrop for the Königin der Nacht in the 1816 production of Mozart's The Magic Flute, which modern productions still quote.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s defeat Schinkel oversaw the Prussian Building Commission, which made him responsible for reshaping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> into a representative capital for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and for projects in the expanded Prussian lands, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> in the west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> in the east. The Prussian royal family patronised him extensively: he produced designs for Stolzenfels Castle, completed Charlottenhof Palace for King Frederick William IV, and worked on Babelsberg Palace and Glienicke Palace for the king's brothers. He died in Berlin on 9 October 1841, at the age of sixty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In his most productive period Schinkel turned to Greek rather than Imperial Roman models, an attempt to move away from the style associated with the recent French occupiers. He held that a building needs elements of the poetic and the past to avoid sterility. His buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> include the Neue Wache (1816–1818), the National Monument for the Liberation Wars (1818–1821) and the Schauspielhaus (1819–1821), which replaced a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> destroyed by fire in 1817. Later he widened his range with the Neo-Gothic Friedrichswerder Church (1824–1831) and the brick Bauakademie (1832–1836), which some see as a forerunner of modern architecture.</p><p>He is noted as much for theory and unbuilt projects as for his relatively few executed buildings. These include plans to turn the Athenian Acropolis into a royal palace for the new Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and to build the Orianda Palace in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, published in his Sammlung architektonischer Entwürfe (1820–1837). He also designed the Prussian Iron Cross medal. His portrait appeared on the 1,000-mark banknote of the Reichsbank from 1936 to 1945.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karl Friedrich Schinkel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
